WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.377
This slide summarizes the strategy behind the more advanced coding exercises.

00:00:04.377 --> 00:00:11.772
The target is one row per patient with three measures: admission count, lab count, and prescription count.

00:00:11.772 --> 00:00:15.077
Patients with no activity must still remain.

00:00:15.077 --> 00:00:20.362
The safest approach is to summarize each child table by patient ID first.

00:00:20.362 --> 00:00:25.917
After aggregation, each child summary contains at most one row per patient.

00:00:25.917 --> 00:00:29.252
Then we LEFT JOIN those summaries to the patient master.

00:00:29.252 --> 00:00:36.412
Because patients is preserved, a patient with no admissions, labs, or prescriptions still appears.

00:00:36.412 --> 00:00:43.192
COALESCE converts a missing count from NULL to zero, which is usually easier to interpret in a report.

00:00:43.192 --> 00:00:45.347
This is a common production pattern.

00:00:45.347 --> 00:00:51.232
A customer dashboard may combine order count, support-ticket count, and refund count.

00:00:51.232 --> 00:00:57.582
A student dashboard may combine registration count, advising visits, and submissions.

00:00:57.582 --> 00:01:02.172
Pre-aggregating each activity source protects the target grain.

00:01:02.172 --> 00:01:06.977
When you run the query, validate at least one patient with multiple activities and one patient with no

00:01:06.977 --> 00:01:12.833
activity. The output row count should equal the number of patients.
